  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "A lump of clay on a potter's wheel"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a lump of clay on a pottery wheel."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "地之吐生物者也。二象地之下、地之中，物出形也。凡土之屬皆从土。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形): a lump of clay either on the ground or on a potter’s wheel. See the bottom part of the original version of 生 and 丰. Then, the lump of clay was stylized as a vertical stroke with a black dot in the middle perhaps to avoid confusion with other characters. Eventually, the black dot turned into a short horizontal stroke. Compare the top part of 土 with the ancient version of 十, which is a vertical stroke with a black dot in the middle.\nUnrelated to the top component of 夌, 走, and 周; also unrelated to the central component of 金 and the overall character 圭.\nThere is no obvious cognate and etymology, unless it may be related to Proto-Austroasiatic *tiʔ (Proto-Monic *tiiʔ (“soil; ground consisting of earth”), Semnam tɛʔ (“soil; earth”)). However, this would require the assumption of a shift from front vowel to Old Chinese *a (Schuessler, 2007).\nProbably related to 社 (OC *ɦljaːʔ, “god of soil; altar to god of soil”)."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "地之吐生物者也。二象地之下、地之中，物出形也。凡土之屬皆从土。",
  "shuowenExplanation": "吐生物者 of earth. 下、地之中 of 二象地; Thing to go out form. All characters in the 土 category contain 土 as radical.",
